Among these methods, knowledge distillation(KD) \cite{arxivID-1503.02531-5} is a popular training strategy for NAT. The idea is to construct synthetic data by using an autoregressive model as the teacher. The synthetic data consists of the input sentence and the corresponding translation generated by the teacher model. The NAT model is then trained on the synthetic data by minimizing the cross-entropy loss between the model's prediction and the ground truth. By doing so, the NAT model can be trained to mimic the teacher model and achieve comparable performance to the teacher model.

However, the KD-based NAT model may suffer from the problem of low-frequency word prediction \cite{arxivID-2106.00903-6}. The synthetic data contains more low-frequency words than the original data, and the KD-based NAT model may have difficulty in predicting these low-frequency words. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig1}, the low-frequency source word \textit{`Zentrum'} is correctly translated by the autoregressive model but incorrectly translated by the KD model. This problem may limit the performance of the NAT model.

To alleviate the problem, we propose to rejuvenate low-frequency words by reverse KD. The idea is to construct synthetic data by using the NAT model as the teacher. The synthetic data consists of the input sentence and the corresponding translation generated by the NAT model. The NAT model is then trained on the synthetic data by minimizing the cross-entropy loss between the model's prediction and the ground truth. By doing so, the NAT model can be trained to mimic itself and achieve better performance on low-frequency words.

Our approach is based on the observation that the alignments of low-frequency source words are more deterministic in the distilled data than in the original data. This is because the KD model is trained to predict the most probable translation for each input sentence, and the low-frequency words are less likely to appear in the most probable translation. As a result, the alignments of low-frequency source words are more deterministic in the distilled data than in the original data. We also found that the alignments of low-frequency target words are less deterministic in the distilled data than in the original data. This is because the KD model is trained to predict the most probable translation for each input sentence, and the low-frequency words are less likely to appear in the most probable translation. As a result, the alignments of low-frequency target words are less deterministic in the distilled data than in the original data.

