To address this problem, a common practice is to train NAT models with a large-scale synthetic dataset constructed by knowledge distillation (KD) \cite{arxivID-1506.02256-1}. The synthetic data generated by KD is usually larger than the original dataset, and it can also be regarded as an expansion of the original dataset. However, the synthetic data generated by KD may contain some errors, and the quality of the synthetic data is not always better than the original data. Therefore, how to utilize the synthetic data effectively and how to make the most of the original data have become two important problems for NAT models.

In this paper, we propose a new training strategy for NAT models to solve these two problems. Our approach consists of two components: pretraining and reverse KD. The pretraining component aims to make the model learn more general knowledge from the original data, while the reverse KD component aims to correct the errors in the synthetic data and make it more reliable. To achieve this, we propose a new KD method that reverses the direction of KD. Instead of using the NAT model to generate synthetic data, we use the autoregressive model to generate synthetic data. This synthetic data can be used to correct the errors in the original data and make it more reliable. By combining pretraining and reverse KD, we can effectively utilize the original data and the synthetic data to improve the performance of NAT models.

We conduct experiments on five translation benchmarks over two advanced architectures. The results demonstrate that the proposed approach can significantly and universally improve translation quality by reducing translation errors on low-frequency words. Encouragingly, our approach achieves 28.2 and 33.9 BLEU points on the WMT14 English-German and WMT16 Romanian-English datasets, respectively. Our code, data, and trained models are available at \url{https://github.com/alphadl/RLFW-NAT}.

The main contributions of our paper are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a new training strategy for NAT models that can effectively utilize the original data and the synthetic data to improve the performance of NAT models.
\item We conduct experiments on five translation benchmarks over two advanced architectures. The results demonstrate that the proposed approach can significantly and universally improve translation quality by reducing translation errors on low-frequency words.
\item We achieve state-of-the-art performance on five translation benchmarks, which demonstrates the effectiveness of our approach.
\end{itemize}
