 Chronic illnesses typically involve multiple healthcare specialists over an extended period. Relying on Electronic Health Records (EHR) alone to understand the health status of a person with a chronic illness could result in an incomplete picture of patient profiles #ref1#. Such an incomplete picture is not only due to disconnected EHR systems being used by different healthcare providers, but  also due to the fact that the EHR systems capture only the formal medical information about the patient and not the patient's daily activities and lifestyle preferences. As a result, the current EHR systems are unable to provide patients with personalized health recommendations that are informed by their social determinants of health (SDoH) and daily living.

To address this challenge, we propose a knowledge model called the Personal Health Ontology (PHO), which is grounded in semantic technologies, and represents a patients combined medical information, SDoH, and observations of daily living elicited from interviews with diabetic patients. We further generate a personal health knowledge graph that captures temporal patterns from synthetic food logs, annotated with concepts from the Personal Health Ontology. We then discuss how lifestyle guidelines grounded in semantic technologies can be reasoned with the generated personal health knowledge graph to provide appropriate dietary recommendations that satisfy the users medical and other lifestyle needs.
