\subsection{Semantic Technologies for Healthcare}
Semantic technologies have been explored in the healthcare domain for knowledge representation and reasoning to provide evidence-based clinical decision support \cite{arxivID-2008.07432-0}. The need for a semantic representation of medical data has been recognized for a long time \cite{arxivID-2008.07432-1}. In 2005, the National Center for Biomedical Ontology (NCBO) was founded with the objective of developing and maintaining a repository of biomedical ontologies and tools to integrate biomedical data. A few years later, the National Library of Medicine (NLM) launched the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) Metathesaurus, which is a collection of 161 biomedical ontologies. In 2009, the National Institute of Health (NIH) launched the Semantic Web Health Care and Life Sciences Interest Group (HCLSIG). More recently, the National Institute of Health (NIH) launched the Semantic Kitchen (SK) initiative, which aims to develop semantic technologies to represent, integrate, and reason with data from a variety of sources to facilitate biomedical research and clinical decision making. In 2017, the National Institute of Health (NIH) launched the National Center for Biomedical Computing (NCBC) program to develop computational methods and tools to extract, integrate, and reason with biomedical data from a variety of sources to facilitate biomedical research and clinical decision making.

\subsection{Ontologies for Health}
There have been numerous efforts to represent the knowledge in the biomedical domain using ontologies, including the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) \cite{arxivID-2109.13348-2}, the Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine (SNOMED) \cite{arxivID-0712.1994-3}, the Gene Ontology \cite{arxivID-1608.03672-4}, and the Human Phenotype Ontology \cite{arxivID-1705.03998-5}. These ontologies are often used to represent medical concepts and their relationships, which can be used to support clinical decision making and to enable interoperability between different healthcare systems. However, these ontologies often suffer from the problem of data sparsity, which can limit their effectiveness in capturing the complex relationships between medical concepts. To address this problem, researchers have proposed the use of knowledge graphs to represent medical concepts and their relationships. A knowledge graph is a type of semantic network that represents the relationships between entities in a knowledge domain. Knowledge graphs have been used in the biomedical domain to represent the relationships between medical concepts, such as diseases, symptoms, and medications \cite{arxivID-2110.13047-6}. In this paper, we propose a knowledge graph-based approach to represent medical concepts and their relationships, which can be used to support clinical decision making and to enable interoperability between different healthcare systems. <|section_end|>

 In recent years, the size of datasets have grown exponentially as a result of advances in technology, signal acquisition, and the sophistication of modern day mobile phones and devices. This has enabled researchers, statisticians and machine learning practitioners to build increasingly accurate models as a consequence of larger sample sizes and feature dimensions. Nevertheless, this poses a fundamental challenge to large scale learning as (i) traditional algorithms have computational complexity that scales with the order of the dataset dimensions (ii) the whole dataset has to be stored or transferred on to local RAM as optimisation  algorithms are iterative in nature. This is particularly relevant in the context of mobile devices and the Internet of Things (IoT) where the memory footprint and computational complexity are critical. Furthermore, the communication cost of transmitting large datasets is a key challenge in the context of large scale learning in sensor networks and edge computing. Therefore, there is a strong need to develop novel algorithms that can reduce the memory footprint of the learning task without significant loss in statistical efficiency.

In this paper, we look at independent component analysis (ICA) through the lens of compressive learning \cite{arxivID-1910.10024-0, arxivID-1910.10024-1}. The aim of this paper is to develop compressive learning algorithms for the nonparametric ICA model. In particular, we show that the ICA model has a low dimensional model set that resides in the cumulant tensor space. By exploiting this structure, we show that the sketch size is proportional to the intrinsic complexity of the model set. Thereafter, we propose two algorithms that are of the form of an iterative projection gradient (IPG) and an alternating steepest descent (ASD) algorithm for compressive ICA. We provide analysis of the CICA algorithms including the effects of finite samples. Finally, we conclude the paper with open problems.

